airfountainsystem.org,0,"Air Fountain System | Official Website Air Fountain System is John Gilmore&#x27;s step-by-step DIY guide (PDF blueprint + videos) to building a generator that condenses clean drinking water from air. $39 one-time. Air Fountain System | Official Website Air Fountain System Home Review Features Pricing FAQ Testimonials Get Instant Access By John Gilmore · Digital DIY Blueprint Air Fountain System Step-by-Step Guide to Building Your Own Air Fountain Water Generator Get Air Fountain System Now Instant PDF Download · 60-day money-back guarantee 4.8 average from 1,263 owner ratings Instant PDF Download 60-day money-back guarantee Build it in a single day One payment of $39 The Short Answer What Is an Air Fountain System? The Air Fountain System is a digital, do-it-yourself program from survival writer John Gilmore that walks an absolute beginner through building a small water generator at home. Nothing arrives in a box. What you buy is the thinking behind the machine, delivered as a downloadable PDF guide and video course: the parts list, the measurements, the assembly order, the mistakes worth skipping and video footage of each stage being put together by hand. The guide contains two builds rather than one. The compact unit suits a balcony, a garage corner or a camper. The larger version is for households that want a meaningful daily volume. Both use ordinary components, both are described from first bolt to first glass, and most people finish either one inside a single day. Think of it as a dehumidifier with a purpose. A dehumidifier pulls moisture out of a room and throws it away. This build pulls the same moisture out of the air and keeps it, filters it and stores it. Air is never truly dry — even over desert country the atmosphere carries an enormous suspended volume of water. Chill it far enough and that water has nowhere to go but down, into a container you control. The Mechanics How Does an Air Fountain System Work? If you have ever watched a cold glass sweat on a summer porch, you already understand the principle. Those droplets did not leak through the glass. They were pulled out of the surrounding air the moment it touched a surface colder than its dew point. The build simply does that on purpose, at a useful scale, and catches everything that runs off. 1 Draw the air in A fan moves ambient air through the unit. Humid air is your raw material, so warm mornings, coastal weather and monsoon months are the strongest conditions. 2 Drop it below dew point The cooling stage chills that incoming air until the vapour it carries can no longer stay suspended and beads onto the collection surface. 3 Collect the condensate Gravity finishes the job. Droplets merge, run down the channel and drip into a sealed vessel instead of evaporating straight back into the room. 4 Filter and mineralise Fresh condensate is close to distilled: clean, but flat and mineral-poor. The bonus reports cover polishing it into water that tastes like water. Output is set by physics, not marketing. Humidity and temperature decide your yield on any given day, and the guide says so plainly rather than promising a fixed number of gallons every morning. Everything for a single $39 payment Blueprint, videos and three bonus reports · 60-day money-back guarantee Get Air Fountain System Now Terminology The Air Fountain Water Extractor “Water extractor” is the phrase people reach for when they want to describe what the finished machine actually does: it extracts water that was already hanging in the air around your house. It belongs to the same family as the commercial atmospheric water generators sold for thousands, stripped back to components an ordinary person can buy and bolt together without a workshop. The distinction matters because it changes where your water comes from. A well depends on an aquifer. A barrel depends on the sky opening — rainwater harvesting has served households for centuries precisely because it works when it rains. A municipal line depends on infrastructure that, as the American Society of Civil Engineers set out in its reporting on America's water crisis , is ageing faster than it is being replaced. An extractor depends only on humid air, and humid a"
