gallowayglens.org,0,"The Galloway Glens Landscape Partnership The Galloway Glens Landscape Partnership Sign up for our Newsletter The Galloway Glens Newsletter is run through Mailchimp. Sign Up to keep up to date with all Galloway Glens news Email address: I have read and agree to the terms & conditions Signing up for this form will add you to our newsletter mailing list on Mailchimp. For more information please read Mailchimp's GDPR Policy Leave this field empty if you're human: Sign up for our Newsletter The Galloway Glens Newsletter is run through Mailchimp. Sign Up to keep up to date with all Galloway Glens news Email address: I have read and agree to the terms & conditions Signing up for this form will add you to our newsletter mailing list on Mailchimp. For more information please read Mailchimp's GDPR Policy Leave this field empty if you're human: Sign up for our Newsletter Sign up for our Newsletter Check out our latest news Home About Project Area Partnership Board Working Groups Staff Projects News Get Involved Resources Events FAQ Contact Home Projects Get Involved Sitemap Welcome Flowing from the wild uplands behind the Glenkens, past the beautiful Loch Ken and the historic towns of Castle Douglas and Kirkcudbright before reaching the Solway Firth, the Galloway Glens project area in South West Scotland is home to a fascinating mix of natural and man-made environments. One of the first hydroelectric schemes in Scotland sits alongside the largest Forest Park in the UK. The UK’s first Dark Sky Park and Scotland’s first UNESCO-designated Galloway and Southern Ayrshire Biosphere are two of our unique features. Focussing on this area within the Ken and Dee river catchments, a series of projects is underway that will connect people and communities with the unique natural and cultural heritage of this area. Funded by the National Lottery Heritage Fund with £2.7million, matched funding will increase the total amount of investment into the area to more than £5million over 2018 - 2023. On this site, you will find many resources to help you better understand the Scheme. The projects are organised into six Themes, and on the following pages you can explore each Theme in more detail, as well as see how the Galloway Glens came into being and benefit from the many resources that the Scheme has created over both the Development and Delivery phases. The video to the right is an excellent introduction to the Scheme, created at the beginning of the Delivery phase in 2018. Galloway Glens Newsletter Sign up for the Galloway Glens Newsletter to keep up to date with Galloway Glens news and events. It usually comes out monthly and you can unsubscribe at any time. Sign up for our Newsletter Themes Heritage Hubs Increase use and therefore sustainability of the community buildings in the area, supporting the built heritage. Visiting Encourage people to visit the area and engage with the heritage. Education & Skills Support sustainable communties communities through education and heritage skills training. Natural Landscapes Support the habitats and species of the area and mitigate negative environmental impacts. Accessing Improving access to the Natural Heritage of the area through a programme of footpath improvement and creation. Understanding To better record and understand our Natural, Cultural and Built Heritage. SMALL GRANTS SCHEME News Local Young People see their work on the wall at the ‘Fantastic Tree for the Future’ Exhibition From the 26th to 29th of May, more than 100 drawings by pupils from Kells, Dalry, Castle Douglas and Kirkcudbright Primary Schools were on display in the Castle Douglas Art Gallery. This was part of the Fantastic Forest Festival held earlier in the year that sought to connect and inspire people of all ages about… Read more » Threave Landscape Restoration Project inspires change Scotland’s largest conservation charity, the National Trust for Scotland, is inspiring change through the 100-year Threave Landscape Restoration Project. Located in the heart of Dumfries and Gallowa"
